Vocabulary Arcade — Sound Bible v1.0
1. Core Audio Identity

Overall style:
Bright, modern arcade + educational technology.

Emotional target:
Fun, rewarding, energetic, polished, never childish.

Avoid:
Realistic weapons, harsh alarms, horror sounds, excessively loud explosions, casino-like effects, cartoon voices, or sounds that could embarrass a student for getting an answer wrong.

Audio hierarchy

Sounds should fall into five levels:

Level 1 — Micro
Tiny interface feedback.
0.05–0.20 sec.

Level 2 — Gameplay
Correct, incorrect, match, pop, gem clear, etc.
0.10–0.40 sec.

Level 3 — Achievement
Streaks, combos, bonuses, level-ups.
0.30–0.80 sec.

Level 4 — Major Event
Game start, time-up, damage, major explosion.
0.50–1.0 sec.

Level 5 — Completion
Victory / successful completion.
1.0–1.5 sec.

The louder and longer a sound is, the less frequently it should occur.

2. The Core Pitch Language

To make the entire platform sound related, positive sounds should primarily use:

C – D – E – G – A

This is essentially a bright pentatonic vocabulary.

The most important positive interval is:

C → E

That becomes the Vocabulary Arcade "correct" sound identity.

For increasingly important achievements, we build upward:

Correct:
C5 → E5

Match:
E5 → G5

Streak:
C5 → E5 → G5

Victory:
C5 → E5 → G5 → C6

Negative sounds should generally descend rather than use the same musical vocabulary.

3. Master Sound Specifications
VA-01 — UI Click

Filename: ui-click-1.mp3

Purpose: Buttons, menus, navigation.

Duration: 0.06–0.10 sec

Sound: Tiny clean digital click/tick.

Pitch: Approximately C6–E6

Attack: Immediate

Decay: Very fast

Volume: Low

Frequency: Very high

Personality: Neutral

The user should barely notice this sound consciously.

VA-02 — Game Start

Filename: game-start.mp3

Purpose: Game becomes active.

Duration: 0.5–0.7 sec

Sound: Three-note ascending digital chime.

Notes: C5 → E5 → G5

Rhythm: Even, quick.

Volume: Medium

Personality: "Let's play."

This becomes one of Vocabulary Arcade's recurring signatures.

4. Answer Sounds
VA-03 — Correct

Filenames:

correct-1.mp3
correct-2.mp3
correct-3.mp3

Duration: 0.15–0.22 sec

Notes: C5 → E5

Sound: Bright electronic chime.

Attack: Very fast

Decay: Fast

Volume: Medium

Variations: Same basic sound, tiny pitch/timbre differences.

Personality: Immediate positive reinforcement.

This is probably the single most important sound in the entire library.

VA-04 — Incorrect

Filenames:

incorrect-1.mp3
incorrect-2.mp3

Duration: 0.20–0.30 sec

Sound: Soft electronic buzz.

Pitch: E4 → C4

Wave character: Slightly rough/square.

Volume: Low-medium

Personality: Informative, not punishing.

It should communicate:

"That wasn't correct."

not:

"YOU FAILED."

5. Matching Sounds
VA-05 — Match

Filename: match.mp3

Duration: 0.25–0.35 sec

Notes: E5 → G5

Sound: Crystal/digital two-note chime.

Volume: Medium

Personality: Satisfying.

Used especially in MatchWord.

6. BubbleWord Sounds
VA-06 — Bubble Pop

Filenames:

bubble-pop-1.mp3
bubble-pop-2.mp3
bubble-pop-3.mp3
bubble-pop-4.mp3

Duration: 0.10–0.18 sec

Sound: Bright, soft bubble pop.

Pitch: Slight variation between versions.

Important: No giant cartoon "POP."

It should feel like a clean arcade interaction.

7. GemWords Sounds
VA-07 — Gem Clear

Filenames:

gem-clear-1.mp3
gem-clear-2.mp3
gem-clear-3.mp3
gem-clear-4.mp3

Duration: 0.20–0.35 sec

Sound: Crystal sparkle + tiny digital chime.

Pitch: Higher than BubbleWord.

Volume: Medium.

Personality: Jewel-like, elegant, satisfying.

VA-08 — Gem Combo

Filenames:

gem-combo-3.mp3
gem-combo-4.mp3
gem-combo-5.mp3
gem-combo-6.mp3

Duration: 0.30–0.65 sec

The larger the combo, the higher the musical movement.

Example:

3 gems: C5 → E5

4 gems: C5 → E5 → G5

5 gems: C5 → E5 → G5 → A5

6+: C5 → E5 → G5 → A5 → C6

This makes the sound itself communicate the size of the achievement.

8. Reward Sounds
VA-09 — Coin / Reward

Filenames:

coin-1.mp3
coin-2.mp3

Duration: 0.15–0.25 sec

Sound: Classic two-tone digital reward.

Notes: E5 → G5

Volume: Medium.

Use this when the player receives something tangible:

points, bonus, ammo, reward, etc.

VA-10 — Streak Bonus

Filename: streak-bonus.mp3

Duration: 0.45–0.65 sec

Notes: C5 → E5 → G5 → A5

Sound: Rising arcade arpeggio.

Volume: Medium-high.

This is what tells the student:

"You're on a roll."

VA-11 — Level Up

Filename: level-up.mp3

Duration: 0.5–0.8 sec

Sound: Rising synth sweep followed by a chime.

Pitch: Low → high

Volume: Medium-high.

Used when BlastWord advances a wave or another game reaches a major progression point.

9. Major Game Events
VA-12 — Explosion

Filenames:

explosion-1.mp3
explosion-2.mp3

Duration: 0.30–0.55 sec

Sound: Arcade explosion.

Character: Punchy low thump + electronic burst.

Avoid: Realistic explosions or gunfire.

Volume: Medium-high.

BlastWord's signature action sound.

VA-13 — Damage

Filename: damage.mp3

Duration: 0.15–0.25 sec

Sound: Low electronic impact.

Pitch: Low.

Volume: Medium.

This is distinct from incorrect.

Incorrect: "Your answer was wrong."

Damage: "Your ship got hit."

VA-14 — Shield Hit

Filename: shield-hit.mp3

Duration: 0.20–0.30 sec

Sound: Metallic/electronic deflection.

Character: Short impact + shimmering discharge.

This should actually feel slightly positive because the shield protected the player.

VA-15 — Laser Fire

Filenames:

laser-1.mp3
laser-2.mp3

Duration: 0.08–0.15 sec

Sound: Bright sci-fi zap.

Pitch: High.

Volume: Medium-low.

Important: it must remain pleasant when fired repeatedly.

10. Timing Sounds
VA-16 — Countdown Tick

Filename: countdown.mp3

Duration: 0.08–0.12 sec

Sound: Tiny digital tick.

Pitch: Medium-high.

Volume: Low.

Use sparingly, preferably only near the end of a timed round.

VA-17 — Time Up

Filename: time-up.mp3

Duration: 0.50–0.75 sec

Notes: G4 → E4 → C4

Sound: Three descending digital notes.

Volume: Medium.

Distinct from Game Over.

11. End States
VA-18 — Victory

Filename: victory.mp3

Duration: 1.0–1.4 sec

Notes:

C5 → E5 → G5 → C6

Then a tiny sparkle/chime.

Sound: Bright arcade fanfare.

Volume: Medium-high.

This should become the signature Vocabulary Arcade completion sound.

When a student completes a 20-word set, this is what they hear.

VA-19 — Game Over

Filename: game-over.mp3

Duration: 0.7–1.0 sec

Notes: E5 → C5 → A4

Sound: Descending electronic phrase.

Volume: Medium.

Not sad or humiliating—just clearly signaling the end.

12. Other Game-Specific Sounds
VA-20 — Card Flip

Filename: card-flip.mp3

Duration: 0.10–0.20 sec

Sound: Short digital/paper swish.

Used primarily by FlashWord.

VA-21 — Drag / Drop

Filenames:

drag-pickup.mp3
drag-drop.mp3

Pickup: 0.08–0.12 sec

Drop: 0.12–0.20 sec

The drop sound should be the satisfying one.

Used by SortWord.

13. Sound Variants

I don't recommend making four versions of everything.

The variation should focus on sounds that students hear repeatedly:

correct-1
correct-2
correct-3

incorrect-1
incorrect-2

bubble-pop-1
bubble-pop-2
bubble-pop-3
bubble-pop-4

gem-clear-1
gem-clear-2
gem-clear-3
gem-clear-4

coin-1
coin-2

laser-1
laser-2

Everything else can be a single carefully designed sound.

That gives us 31 total assets while maintaining a manageable library.

14. Volume Hierarchy

This is particularly important for a classroom platform.

I would establish this maximum hierarchy:

Sound Type	Relative Volume
UI click	Very low
Countdown	Very low
Card flip	Low
Correct	Medium
Incorrect	Medium
Match	Medium
Bubble pop	Medium
Gem clear	Medium
Coin	Medium
Laser	Medium
Damage	Medium
Streak	Medium-high
Level up	Medium-high
Explosion	Medium-high
Victory	Highest

The victory sound gets the biggest impact.

A student completing the final word should feel different from answering question #3 correctly.

15. Which Sound Goes With Which Event?

Here's the master mapping I would use in the games.

Event	Sound
Click button	ui-click
Start game	game-start
Correct answer	correct-*
Wrong answer	incorrect-*
Successful pairing	match
Pop bubble	bubble-pop-*
Clear gems	gem-clear-*
Gem combo	gem-combo-*
Earn points/reward	coin-*
Build streak	streak-bonus
New wave	level-up
Blast enemy	explosion-*
Fire weapon	laser-*
Player damaged	damage
Shield blocks hit	shield-hit
Time warning	countdown
Time expires	time-up
Flip flashcard	card-flip
Pick up draggable item	drag-pickup
Drop draggable item	drag-drop
Win/complete	victory
Lose/end	game-over
16. One Important Rule for the Code

I would also establish this as part of the sound bible:

Gameplay code should never directly manipulate audio files.

Instead of:

new Audio("sounds/correct.mp3").play();

we'll eventually use:

SoundFX.correct();

and:

SoundFX.explosion();

That means the Sound Bible becomes the specification, while sounds.js becomes its implementation.

This will make it possible to change the audio library later without rewriting eight games.

